# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists: this repo is developed on Windows, where git is commonly
# configured with `core.autocrlf=true`. That rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout -
# and a shell script with CRLF line endings fails in ways that read as nonsense,
# because the trailing \r becomes part of each command:
#
#     scripts/verify.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#
# The same applies to anything executed inside a Linux container. The Dockerfile,
# the workflow YAML, and the ignore files are all read by Linux tooling that does
# not expect CRLF.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository AND in the working tree,
# so the files are byte-identical on every machine regardless of each developer's
# autocrlf setting. This is the kind of bug that only appears on someone else's
# clone, which makes it worth pinning rather than discovering.

# Default: let git detect text files, and check them out with LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, because these are the ones that actually break:
*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
.trivyignore  text eol=lf

# The exception that proves the rule: cmd.exe is the one interpreter that wants
# CRLF. The blanket `eol=lf` above would otherwise apply to batch files too, and
# an LF-only .cmd misbehaves around labels and goto - the same class of bug as a
# CRLF .sh, just pointing the other way.
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Binary - never touch these.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.ico       binary
